<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">November Uprising</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1831">1831</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The November Uprising was a Polish revolt against Russian rule in Congress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> that began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> on 29 November 1830 with an attack by officer cadets on the Belweder Palace. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> deposed Nicholas I as king of Poland in January 1831, but the Polish army was defeated and Warsaw fell in September 1831. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> abolished the kingdom's constitution, and thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> left in the Great Emigration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/november-uprising.jpg" alt="November Uprising" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcin Zaleski · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marcin_Zaleski,_Wzi%C4%99cie_Arsena%C5%82u.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> in 1815 under Russian control, in personal union with the tsar as king. It had its own constitution, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a>, courts, army and treasury, but the freedoms were gradually taken back. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> introduced censorship in 1819, Russian secret police persecuted Polish clandestine organisations, and sessions of the Sejm were held in secret after 1825. Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-konstantin-pavlovich-of-russia">Constantine Pavlovich</a>, the de facto viceroy, abolished Polish social and patriotic organisations and replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> with Russians in important posts. The final spark was a Russian plan to use the Polish army against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a>, in violation of the constitution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 29 November 1830 a group led by the cadet Piotr Wysocki took arms from their garrison and attacked the Belweder Palace, but Constantine escaped. Armed civilians forced the Russian troops to withdraw the next day. General Józef Chłopicki was proclaimed dictator on 5 December 1830. He hoped to negotiate, but Tsar Nicholas I demanded unconditional surrender, and radicals around Joachim Lelewel took power. On 25 January 1831 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> passed the Act of Dethronization of Nicholas I.</p><p>On 4 February 1831 a Russian army of 115,000 under Field Marshal Hans Karl von Diebitsch crossed the border. Polish cavalry won at Stoczek, and the bloody battle of Olszynka Grochowska on 25 February saved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> for a time. Frequent changes of command, and hopes of foreign help that never came, weakened the Polish side. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> did not intervene, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> closed their frontiers to supplies. After Diebitsch died, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-paskevich">Ivan Paskevich</a> moved against Warsaw, and its suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wola">Wola</a> fell on 6 September 1831. On 5 October the remaining army of over 20,000 men crossed into Prussia and laid down its arms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 1832 Nicholas I issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regulamentul-organic">Organic Statute</a>, under which Russian-occupied Poland lost its autonomy and became an integral part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. Warsaw became little more than a military garrison, and its university closed. Thousands of insurgents were executed, imprisoned or forced into exile in what became known as the Great Emigration, which included many prominent intellectuals and artists.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had sent over 180,000 trained men against about 70,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, yet the war lasted eight months. Critics at the time and later blamed divided leadership, hesitation and the failure to move the fighting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Popular sympathy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was strong in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, but governments held to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> system and the Polish cause remained diplomatically isolated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “November Uprising” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Discontent at the rule of Nicholas I broke into the <b>November Uprising</b>: when the Sejm deposed him as King of Poland in January 1831, he sent Russian troops in, and the insurrection was defeated after an 11-month military campaign.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Franz Schubert set more than six hundred poems to music, and the Polish pianist Frédéric Chopin, who settled in Paris in 1831 as the <b>November Uprising</b> in Poland was crushed, turned the memory of his country into mazurkas and polonaises.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The title once carried honour: Giuseppe Garibaldi officially styled himself “Dictator” of Sicily during the Expedition of the Thousand in 1860, and leaders of Poland’s <b>November Uprising</b> of 1830 and January Uprising of 1863 held the same title.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Through a century of foreign rule after 1815 it was sung in every national uprising; in the <b>November Uprising</b> of 1830–1831 it was chanted on the battlefields, and at least 16 new versions were written.</blockquote><a href="/songs/poland-is-not-yet-lost">Poland Is Not Yet Lost</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>November Uprising</b> of 1830 was crushed, and Marshal Ivan Paskevich governed as viceroy for decades.</blockquote><a href="/state/congress-poland">Congress Poland</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>November Uprising</b> of 1830 in Congress Poland, the provincial president Eduard Flottwell began a policy of Germanisation.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-posen">Grand Duchy of Posen</a></li></ul></section>
